DevOps Lab Experiments 17--28
Docker, Kubernetes and Selenium --- Step-by-Step Commands and Programs
Command environment: These instructions use Windows Command Prompt (CMD). Open CMD for the commands and keep Docker Desktop running for Docker and Kubernetes experiments.
Important before starting - Start Docker Desktop and wait until Docker Engine shows Running before running Docker commands. - For Kubernetes experiments, enable Kubernetes in Docker Desktop Settings → Kubernetes, then wait until the cluster is ready. - Commands using %cd% are for Windows CMD, not PowerShell. - When a command opens Notepad, paste the given code, save the file with the exact filename, and close Notepad. - If a folder already exists, use cd FolderName instead of creating it again. - Docker commands may need docker to be replaced with sudo docker on Linux if your user does not have Docker permissions.
Experiment 17 -- Execute Basic Docker Commands
Aim: To execute basic Docker commands and manage Docker images and containers.
Steps
Start Docker Desktop and wait until Docker Engine is running.
Open Command Prompt.
Check Docker installation:
docker --version
Display Docker information:
docker info
Download the hello-world image:
docker pull hello-world
List downloaded images:
docker images
Run a container:
docker run hello-world
List running containers:
docker ps
List all containers, including stopped ones:
docker ps -a
Run an Ubuntu container:
docker run -it ubuntu bash
Inside the container, type exit to leave it.
To stop a running container, first find its ID or name using docker ps, then run:
docker stop CONTAINER_ID
To remove a stopped container:
docker rm CONTAINER_ID
To remove the hello-world image after removing any container that uses it:
docker rmi hello-world
Expected output: docker run hello-world displays "Hello from Docker!"
Result: Basic Docker commands were executed successfully.
Experiment 18 -- Run a Containerized Application Using Docker
Aim: To create a simple HTML application, build a Docker image and run it in a container.
Step 1: Create a project folder
mkdir DockerApp
cd DockerApp
Step 2: Create the HTML file
notepad index.html
Paste and save:
<html>
<head>
    <title>Docker Application</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Hello from Docker Container!</h1>
    <p>My first containerized application.</p>
</body>
</html>
Step 3: Create a Dockerfile
notepad Dockerfile
Paste and save this file with the exact name Dockerfile (no .txt extension):
FROM nginx:latest
COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
EXPOSE 80
Step 4: Build the image
docker build -t myapp .
Step 5: Check the image
docker images
Step 6: Run the container
docker run -d -p 8080:80 --name mycontainer myapp
Step 7: Check the running container
docker ps
Step 8: View the application
Open a browser and visit:
http://localhost:8080
The page should display Hello from Docker Container!
Step 9: Stop and remove the container
docker stop mycontainer
docker rm mycontainer
Result: The HTML application was built into a Docker image and accessed through a running container.
Experiment 19 -- Execute a Python Program Using Docker
Aim: To create and execute a Python program using Docker.
Step 1: Start Docker Desktop
Wait until Docker Engine shows Running.
Step 2: Create a folder and enter it
mkdir PythonDocker
cd PythonDocker
Step 3: Create the Python file
notepad hello.py
Paste and save:
print("Hello World")
print("Python program executed successfully")
Step 4: Run the Python program inside Docker
Run this command in the same folder:
docker run --rm -v "%cd%:/app" -w /app python:3.12 python hello.py
Expected output:
Hello World
Python program executed successfully
Result: The Python program was successfully executed using Docker.
Note: No separate Dockerfile is required for this method. The python:3.12 image supplies Python inside the container.
Experiment 20 -- Execute a Java Program Using Docker
Aim: To create and execute a Java program using Docker.
Step 1: Create a folder
mkdir JavaDocker
cd JavaDocker
Step 2: Create the Java file
notepad Hello.java
Paste and save:
