Experiment 21 -- Execute a Java Program Using a Parameterized Constructor in Docker
Aim: To demonstrate a parameterized constructor in Java and execute the program using Docker.
Step 1: Create a folder
mkdir Exp21
cd Exp21
Step 2: Create the Java file
notepad Student.java
Paste and save:
class Student {
    int id;
    String name;

    Student(int i, String n) {
        id = i;
        name = n;
    }

    void display() {
        System.out.println("ID: " + id);
        System.out.println("Name: " + name);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s = new Student(101, "Rahul");
        s.display();
    }
}
Step 3: Compile and run using Docker
docker run --rm -v "%cd%:/app" -w /app eclipse-temurin:21-jdk sh -c "javac Student.java && java Student"
Expected output:
ID: 101
Name: Rahul
Result: The Java program using a parameterized constructor was successfully executed using Docker.
Experiment 22 -- Execute a Palindrome Java Program Using Docker
Aim: To check whether a number is a palindrome using Java and Docker.
Step 1: Create a folder
mkdir Exp22
cd Exp22
Step 2: Create the Java file
notepad Palindrome.java
Paste and save:
class Palindrome {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 121;
        int temp = n;
        int rev = 0;

        while (n > 0) {
            int r = n % 10;
            rev = rev * 10 + r;
            n = n / 10;
        }

        if (temp == rev)
            System.out.println("Palindrome");
        else
            System.out.println("Not Palindrome");
    }
}
Step 3: Compile and run using Docker
docker run --rm -v "%cd%:/app" -w /app eclipse-temurin:21-jdk sh -c "javac Palindrome.java && java Palindrome"
Expected output:
Palindrome
Result: The palindrome Java program was successfully executed using Docker.
Experiment 23 -- Execute a Single Inheritance Java Program Using Docker
Aim: To demonstrate single inheritance using Java and Docker.
Step 1: Create a folder
mkdir Exp23
cd Exp23
Step 2: Create the Java file
notepad Inheritance.java
Paste and save:
class Animal {
    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Animal is eating");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    void bark() {
        System.out.println("Dog is barking");
    }
}

class Inheritance {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Dog d = new Dog();
        d.eat();
        d.bark();
    }
}
Step 3: Compile and run using Docker
docker run --rm -v "%cd%:/app" -w /app eclipse-temurin:21-jdk sh -c "javac Inheritance.java && java Inheritance"
Expected output:
Animal is eating
Dog is barking
Result: Single inheritance was demonstrated successfully using Docker.
Experiment 24 -- Integrate Kubernetes with Docker
Aim: To enable Kubernetes in Docker Desktop and check the Kubernetes cluster.
Step 1: Start Docker Desktop
Wait until Docker Engine shows Running.
Step 2: Enable Kubernetes
Open Docker Desktop.
Open Settings.
Select Kubernetes.
Enable Kubernetes.
Select Apply & Restart.
Wait until Kubernetes shows that the cluster is running/ready.
Step 3: Open Command Prompt and check Docker
docker --version
Step 4: Check kubectl
kubectl version --client
Step 5: Check Kubernetes nodes
kubectl get nodes
Expected output: A node such as docker-desktop should show status Ready.
Step 6: Display cluster information
kubectl cluster-info
Result: Kubernetes was enabled in Docker Desktop and the cluster information was displayed.
Note: If Kubernetes is not available in your Docker Desktop version, install and configure a Kubernetes environment such as Minikube instead. Do not continue until kubectl get nodes shows a ready node.
Experiment 25 -- Deploy and Manage a Containerized Application Using Kubernetes
Aim: To deploy and access a containerized application using Kubernetes.
Step 1: Start Docker Desktop
Make sure Docker Engine and Kubernetes are running.
Step 2: Create a deployment
Open Command Prompt:
kubectl create deployment myapp --image=nginx
Step 3: Check the deployment
kubectl get deployments
Step 4: Check pods
kubectl get pods
Wait until the pod status becomes Running.
Step 5: Expose the application
kubectl expose deployment myapp --type=ClusterIP --port=80
Step 6: Check the service
kubectl get services
Step 7: Forward a local port to the application
kubectl port-forward service/myapp 8080:80
Keep this Command Prompt window open while testing.
Step 8: Open the application
Open a browser and visit:
http://localhost:8080
The Nginx welcome page should appear.
Step 9: Stop port forwarding
Press Ctrl + C in the Command Prompt window running port-forward.
Step 10: Delete the service and deployment
kubectl delete service myapp
kubectl delete deployment myapp
Result: The Nginx application was deployed and accessed using Kubernetes.
