Experiment 27 -- Execute a JavaScript Selenium Test
Aim: To automate a browser using Selenium WebDriver and JavaScript.
Step 1: Create a project folder
mkdir Exp27
cd Exp27
Step 2: Initialize Node.js
npm init -y
Step 3: Install Selenium
npm install selenium-webdriver
Step 4: Make sure Google Chrome is installed
Install Google Chrome if it is not already installed. Selenium Manager normally obtains the required Chrome driver automatically when the test runs.
Step 5: Create the JavaScript file
notepad test.js
Paste and save:
const { Builder } = require('selenium-webdriver');

async function test() {
    let driver = await new Builder().forBrowser('chrome').build();

    try {
        await driver.get('https://www.google.com');
        let title = await driver.getTitle();
        console.log("Page Title: " + title);
    } finally {
        await driver.quit();
    }
}

test().catch(console.error);
Step 6: Run the test
node test.js
Expected output:
Page Title: Google
Chrome should open Google, read the page title, and close.
Result: A JavaScript Selenium test was successfully executed.
Experiment 28 -- Test a Containerized Application Using Selenium
Aim: To test a web application running inside a Docker container using Selenium and JavaScript.
Step 1: Start Docker Desktop
Wait until Docker Engine shows Running.
Step 2: Create a project folder
mkdir Exp28
cd Exp28
Step 3: Create an HTML file
notepad index.html
Paste and save:
<html>
<head>
    <title>Docker App</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1 id="message">Hello Docker</h1>
</body>
</html>
Step 4: Run the HTML application in an Nginx container
Run this command from the Exp28 folder:
docker run -d --name testapp -p 8080:80 -v "%cd%:/usr/share/nginx/html:ro" nginx
Step 5: Check the container
docker ps
Step 6: Check the application
Open a browser and visit:
http://localhost:8080
The page should show Hello Docker.
Step 7: Initialize Node.js
Open a second Command Prompt window. Move into the same Exp28 folder, using its actual path, and run:
npm init -y
Step 8: Install Selenium
npm install selenium-webdriver
Make sure Google Chrome is installed.
Step 9: Create the Selenium test file
notepad test.js
Paste and save:
const { Builder, By } = require('selenium-webdriver');

async function test() {
    let driver = await new Builder().forBrowser('chrome').build();

    try {
        await driver.get('http://localhost:8080');

        let title = await driver.getTitle();
        let message = await driver.findElement(By.id('message')).getText();

        console.log("Title: " + title);
        console.log("Message: " + message);
    } finally {
        await driver.quit();
    }
}

test().catch(console.error);
Step 10: Run the Selenium test
node test.js
Expected output:
Title: Docker App
Message: Hello Docker
Step 11: Stop and remove the Docker container
docker stop testapp
docker rm testapp
Result: The containerized HTML application was successfully tested using Selenium WebDriver and JavaScript.
